{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"workflow-adapter-roadmap","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workflow Adapter Roadmap"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is an internal planning note for Workflow adapters. The goal is to avoid\nshipping a large adapter matrix before the reference adapters are boring,\ntested, dogfooded, and deployed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"position","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Position"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapter count is not the win. A small number of correct, well-documented,\ndeployed adapters is more valuable than many adapters with weak durability\nsemantics."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The current quality bar is:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Postgres is the reference SQL adapter."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare D1 is the first non-Postgres pressure test."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Host adapters wake the runtime; they do not own workflow state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Store adapters own their persistence schema/resources and publish setup and\nmigration artifacts."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core stays headless and only depends on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WorkflowExecutionStore"},{"type":"text","value":" contract."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"prove-the-shape-first","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prove the Shape First"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before adding many more adapters, harden these areas:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Area"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What right means"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Store contract"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Covers leases, timers, schedules, signals, approvals, replay, idempotency, pagination, and stale recovery without adapter-specific hacks."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migration ownership"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Package-owned artifacts work in real app deploys. Apps do not mirror "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"workflow_*"},{"type":"text","value":" internals."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Host wakeups"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cron, scheduled functions, worker alarms, and background routes wake bounded sweeps predictably."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Backpressure"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sweeps stay bounded when many timers or schedules are due."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observability"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Users can answer what ran, what is stuck, what is due, and what failed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upgrade story"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Package versions clearly map to required store migrations."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dogfood"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack.com or another real app runs the adapter under real deploy constraints."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"near-term-order","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Near-Term Order"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harden "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is the reference adapter."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every future store should pass the same contract suite."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dogfood it in TanStack.com until migrations, sweeps, schedules, and\nrecovery feel routine."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-store-cloudflare-d1"},{"type":"text","value":" as the non-Postgres pressure\ntest."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"D1 forces SQLite semantics, no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED"},{"type":"text","value":", Cloudflare Worker\nconstraints, and Wrangler migration ergonomics."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Deploy a small Cloudflare Worker demo with Cron Trigger + D1."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Expand the contract suite before adding more stores."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schedule claiming with old started buckets."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Timer claim limits and backpressure."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stale lease recovery."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Duplicate signals and approvals."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Append conflict behavior."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"List/timeline pagination."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Schema migration artifact checks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bounded sweep behavior under load."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Write "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/workflow-runtime/ADAPTER_AUTHORING.md"},{"type":"text","value":" once Postgres and D1\nhave settled."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Required atomic operations."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Acceptable eventual-consistency caveats."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migration artifact requirements."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract test requirements."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production caveats each adapter must document."}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"future-adapter-order","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Future Adapter Order"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After Postgres and D1 survive real deployment:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-node"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Long-running worker/sweeper helpers for Railway, Fly, Docker, and\nself-hosted Node."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-store-libsql"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reuse the SQLite/D1 lessons."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Validate whether a shared SQLite store base is worth extracting."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/workflow-store-redis"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requires careful Lua/atomic script design for claims, leases, idempotency,\ntimers, and schedule buckets."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not rush this one; Redis will reveal weak store-contract assumptions."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare Queues wakeup support."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Useful for draining backlogs and improving timer latency beyond pure cron."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"AWS/DynamoDB/MySQL/GCP adapters."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add only when there is demonstrated demand or dogfood pressure."}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"partner-priority","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partner Priority"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer partner-native documentation and feature polish in this order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cloudflare"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Railway"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Netlify"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vercel compatibility without preferential treatment"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"release-discipline","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Release Discipline"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Any durable store adapter that changes schema, key layout, migration helpers, or\natomic behavior needs a changeset."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production docs should always say:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"apply adapter-owned migrations before deploying the matching adapter version"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keep "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ensureSchema()"},{"type":"text","value":" for tests, local demos, and explicit admin/bootstrap\nscripts"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"never create schema from request handlers, cron ticks, scheduled functions, or\nsweeps"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"decision-rule","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decision Rule"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not add another durable store adapter until at least one existing deployed\nadapter has made the missing requirement obvious."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When in doubt, improve the contract suite, docs, dogfood demo, and observability\nbefore adding a new package."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"workflow-adapter-roadmap","text":"Workflow Adapter Roadmap","level":1},{"id":"position","text":"Position","level":2},{"id":"prove-the-shape-first","text":"Prove the Shape First","level":2},{"id":"near-term-order","text":"Near-Term Order","level":2},{"id":"future-adapter-order","text":"Future Adapter Order","level":2},{"id":"partner-priority","text":"Partner Priority","level":2},{"id":"release-discipline","text":"Release Discipline","level":2},{"id":"decision-rule","text":"Decision Rule","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Workflow Adapter Roadmap\n\nThis is an internal planning note for Workflow adapters. The goal is to avoid\nshipping a large adapter matrix before the reference adapters are boring,\ntested, dogfooded, and deployed.\n\n## Position\n\nAdapter count is not the win. A small number of correct, well-documented,\ndeployed adapters is more valuable than many adapters with weak durability\nsemantics.\n\nThe current quality bar is:\n\n- Postgres is the reference SQL adapter.\n- Cloudflare D1 is the first non-Postgres pressure test.\n- Host adapters wake the runtime; they do not own workflow state.\n- Store adapters own their persistence schema/resources and publish setup and\n  migration artifacts.\n- Core stays headless and only depends on the `WorkflowExecutionStore` contract.\n\n## Prove the Shape First\n\nBefore adding many more adapters, harden these areas:\n\n| Area | What right means |\n| --- | --- |\n| Store contract | Covers leases, timers, schedules, signals, approvals, replay, idempotency, pagination, and stale recovery without adapter-specific hacks. |\n| Migration ownership | Package-owned artifacts work in real app deploys. Apps do not mirror `workflow_*` internals. |\n| Host wakeups | Cron, scheduled functions, worker alarms, and background routes wake bounded sweeps predictably. |\n| Backpressure | Sweeps stay bounded when many timers or schedules are due. |\n| Observability | Users can answer what ran, what is stuck, what is due, and what failed. |\n| Upgrade story | Package versions clearly map to required store migrations. |\n| Dogfood | TanStack.com or another real app runs the adapter under real deploy constraints. |\n\n## Near-Term Order\n\n1. Harden `@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres`.\n   - It is the reference adapter.\n   - Every future store should pass the same contract suite.\n   - Dogfood it in TanStack.com until migrations, sweeps, schedules, and\n     recovery feel routine.\n\n2. Use `@tanstack/workflow-store-cloudflare-d1` as the non-Postgres pressure\n   test.\n   - D1 forces SQLite semantics, no `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, Cloudflare Worker\n     constraints, and Wrangler migration ergonomics.\n   - Deploy a small Cloudflare Worker demo with Cron Trigger + D1.\n\n3. Expand the contract suite before adding more stores.\n   - Schedule claiming with old started buckets.\n   - Timer claim limits and backpressure.\n   - Stale lease recovery.\n   - Duplicate signals and approvals.\n   - Append conflict behavior.\n   - List/timeline pagination.\n   - Schema migration artifact checks.\n   - Bounded sweep behavior under load.\n\n4. Write `packages/workflow-runtime/ADAPTER_AUTHORING.md` once Postgres and D1\n   have settled.\n   - Required atomic operations.\n   - Acceptable eventual-consistency caveats.\n   - Migration artifact requirements.\n   - Contract test requirements.\n   - Production caveats each adapter must document.\n\n## Future Adapter Order\n\nAfter Postgres and D1 survive real deployment:\n\n1. `@tanstack/workflow-node`\n   - Long-running worker/sweeper helpers for Railway, Fly, Docker, and\n     self-hosted Node.\n\n2. `@tanstack/workflow-store-libsql`\n   - Reuse the SQLite/D1 lessons.\n   - Validate whether a shared SQLite store base is worth extracting.\n\n3. `@tanstack/workflow-store-redis`\n   - Requires careful Lua/atomic script design for claims, leases, idempotency,\n     timers, and schedule buckets.\n   - Do not rush this one; Redis will reveal weak store-contract assumptions.\n\n4. Cloudflare Queues wakeup support.\n   - Useful for draining backlogs and improving timer latency beyond pure cron.\n\n5. AWS/DynamoDB/MySQL/GCP adapters.\n   - Add only when there is demonstrated demand or dogfood pressure.\n\n## Partner Priority\n\nPrefer partner-native documentation and feature polish in this order:\n\n1. Cloudflare\n2. Railway\n3. Netlify\n4. Vercel compatibility without preferential treatment\n\n## Release Discipline\n\nAny durable store adapter that changes schema, key layout, migration helpers, or\natomic behavior needs a changeset.\n\nProduction docs should always say:\n\n- apply adapter-owned migrations before deploying the matching adapter version\n- keep `ensureSchema()` for tests, local demos, and explicit admin/bootstrap\n  scripts\n- never create schema from request handlers, cron ticks, scheduled functions, or\n  sweeps\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDo not add another durable store adapter until at least one existing deployed\nadapter has made the missing requirement obvious.\n\nWhen in doubt, improve the contract suite, docs, dogfood demo, and observability\nbefore adding a new package.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Workflow Adapter Roadmap\n\nThis is an internal planning note for Workflow adapters. The goal is to avoid\nshipping a large adapter matrix before the reference adapters are boring,\ntested, dogfooded, and deployed.\n\n## Position\n\nAdapter count is not the win. A small number of correct, well-documented,\ndeployed adapters is more valuable than many adapters with weak durability\nsemantics.\n\nThe current quality bar is:\n\n- Postgres is the reference SQL adapter.\n- Cloudflare D1 is the first non-Postgres pressure test.\n- Host adapters wake the runtime; they do not own workflow state.\n- Store adapters own their persistence schema/resources and publish setup and\n  migration artifacts.\n- Core stays headless and only depends on the `WorkflowExecutionStore` contract.\n\n## Prove the Shape First\n\nBefore adding many more adapters, harden these areas:\n\n| Area | What right means |\n| --- | --- |\n| Store contract | Covers leases, timers, schedules, signals, approvals, replay, idempotency, pagination, and stale recovery without adapter-specific hacks. |\n| Migration ownership | Package-owned artifacts work in real app deploys. Apps do not mirror `workflow_*` internals. |\n| Host wakeups | Cron, scheduled functions, worker alarms, and background routes wake bounded sweeps predictably. |\n| Backpressure | Sweeps stay bounded when many timers or schedules are due. |\n| Observability | Users can answer what ran, what is stuck, what is due, and what failed. |\n| Upgrade story | Package versions clearly map to required store migrations. |\n| Dogfood | TanStack.com or another real app runs the adapter under real deploy constraints. |\n\n## Near-Term Order\n\n1. Harden `@tanstack/workflow-store-drizzle-postgres`.\n   - It is the reference adapter.\n   - Every future store should pass the same contract suite.\n   - Dogfood it in TanStack.com until migrations, sweeps, schedules, and\n     recovery feel routine.\n\n2. Use `@tanstack/workflow-store-cloudflare-d1` as the non-Postgres pressure\n   test.\n   - D1 forces SQLite semantics, no `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, Cloudflare Worker\n     constraints, and Wrangler migration ergonomics.\n   - Deploy a small Cloudflare Worker demo with Cron Trigger + D1.\n\n3. Expand the contract suite before adding more stores.\n   - Schedule claiming with old started buckets.\n   - Timer claim limits and backpressure.\n   - Stale lease recovery.\n   - Duplicate signals and approvals.\n   - Append conflict behavior.\n   - List/timeline pagination.\n   - Schema migration artifact checks.\n   - Bounded sweep behavior under load.\n\n4. Write `packages/workflow-runtime/ADAPTER_AUTHORING.md` once Postgres and D1\n   have settled.\n   - Required atomic operations.\n   - Acceptable eventual-consistency caveats.\n   - Migration artifact requirements.\n   - Contract test requirements.\n   - Production caveats each adapter must document.\n\n## Future Adapter Order\n\nAfter Postgres and D1 survive real deployment:\n\n1. `@tanstack/workflow-node`\n   - Long-running worker/sweeper helpers for Railway, Fly, Docker, and\n     self-hosted Node.\n\n2. `@tanstack/workflow-store-libsql`\n   - Reuse the SQLite/D1 lessons.\n   - Validate whether a shared SQLite store base is worth extracting.\n\n3. `@tanstack/workflow-store-redis`\n   - Requires careful Lua/atomic script design for claims, leases, idempotency,\n     timers, and schedule buckets.\n   - Do not rush this one; Redis will reveal weak store-contract assumptions.\n\n4. Cloudflare Queues wakeup support.\n   - Useful for draining backlogs and improving timer latency beyond pure cron.\n\n5. AWS/DynamoDB/MySQL/GCP adapters.\n   - Add only when there is demonstrated demand or dogfood pressure.\n\n## Partner Priority\n\nPrefer partner-native documentation and feature polish in this order:\n\n1. Cloudflare\n2. Railway\n3. Netlify\n4. Vercel compatibility without preferential treatment\n\n## Release Discipline\n\nAny durable store adapter that changes schema, key layout, migration helpers, or\natomic behavior needs a changeset.\n\nProduction docs should always say:\n\n- apply adapter-owned migrations before deploying the matching adapter version\n- keep `ensureSchema()` for tests, local demos, and explicit admin/bootstrap\n  scripts\n- never create schema from request handlers, cron ticks, scheduled functions, or\n  sweeps\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDo not add another durable store adapter until at least one existing deployed\nadapter has made the missing requirement obvious.\n\nWhen in doubt, improve the contract suite, docs, dogfood demo, and observability\nbefore adding a new package.\n"}
